 be expanded into an absolute path using the skill roots table.\n### Skill roots\n- `r0` = `/Users/kingsonwu/.codex/skills`\n- `r1` = `/Users/kingsonwu/.agents/skills`\n- `r2` = `/Users/kingsonwu/.codex/skills/.system`\n- `r3` = `/Users/kingsonwu/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/figma/20.0.0/skills`\n- `r4` = `/Users/kingsonwu/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/pages/0.1.26/skills`\n- `r5` = `/Users/kingsonwu/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote`\n- `r6` = `/Users/kingsonwu/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/sites/1.1.7/skills`\n- `r7` = `/Users/kingsonwu/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/work-pets/0.1.6/skills`\n- `r8` = `/Users/kingsonwu/programming/OctopusGarage/mesh-talk/.agents/skills`\n### Available skills\n- openai-docs: API first-timers, Codex/Work Q\u0026A. If the user already has an OpenAI API integration, skip this skill for API questions and use the developer docs. Read this skill before answering how Codex or ChatGPT Work behaves, where to find a feature, or how to configure or troubleshoot this app or coding agent. This includes conversation history, model and cache behavior, instruction precedence, permissions, and tool availability. Use for API onboarding only when the user is connecting to the OpenAI API for the first  (file: r2/openai-docs/SKILL.md)\n- skill-creator: Create or update a Codex skill with appropriately scoped instructions and any needed supporting resources. (file: r2/skill-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-installer: Install Codex skills into $CODEX_HOME/skills from a curated list or a GitHub repo path. Use when a user asks to list installable skills, install a curated skill, or install a skill from another repo (including private repos). (file: r2/skill-installer/SKILL.md)\n- animate: Build an animation from scratch, making the decisions in the order that determines whether it feels right — should it animate at all, what purpose, which tool, which properties, which curve and duration, how it interrupts, how it exits. Writes the implementation. Use when asked to animate something, add motion, make a component feel alive, or build a transition. For critiquing existing motion use review-animations; for auditing a whole codebase use improve-animations. (file: r8/animate/SKILL.md)\n- animation-vocabulary: Reverse-lookup glossary that turns a vague description of a web animation or motion effect into its exact term (\"the bouncy thing when a popover opens\" → Pop in; \"the iOS rubber-band scroll\" → Rubber-banding). Use when the user asks \"what's it called when…\", or describes a motion effect without knowing its name and wants the right word to prompt an AI or designer with. For naming an effect, not designing or building one. (file: r8/animation-vocabulary/SKILL.md)\n- apple-design: Apple's approach to interface design and fluid, physical motion, translated for the web. Use when building or reviewing gesture-driven UI, spring animations, drag/swipe/sheet interactions, momentum and interruptible transitions, translucent materials and depth, typography (optical sizing, tracking, leading), reduced-motion, or the design foundations (feedback, spatial consistency, restraint) behind Apple-style interfaces. (file: r8/apple-design/SKILL.md)\n- ask-sonner: Guide to Sonner, the React toast library — install and wire up the Toaster, pick the right toast() call, promise and loading toasts, updating, dismissing and persisting toasts, styling, theming and icons, positioning and multiple toasters. Use when working with Sonner or troubleshooting it — toasts that don't appear, appear twice, lose their styles, ignore Tailwind classes, sit behind a modal, or don't follow dark mode. (file: r8/ask-sonner/SKILL.md)\n- break-ui: Try to break a piece of UI by feeding it worst-case data — long names, unbreakable emails, one-letter names, missing fields, huge counts, zero items, long labels, non-Latin text, emoji, extreme numbers — then render it behind a \"Demo data / Worst case\" toggle and report everything that broke, with the fix for each. Use when the user asks to stress-test, break, or find edge cases in a component or screen, or to \"try the worst case\". For visual design critique use emil-design-eng; for motion use review-anim (file: r8/break-ui/SKILL.md)\n- design-taste-frontend: Anti-slop frontend skill for landing pages, portfolios, and redesigns. The agent reads the brief, infers the right design direction, and ships interfaces that do not look templated. Real design systems when applicable, audit-first on redesigns, strict pre-flight check. (file: r8/design-taste-frontend/SKILL.md)\n- emil-design-eng: This skill encodes Emil Kowalski's philosophy on UI polish, component design, animation decisions, and the invisible details that make software feel great. (file: r8/emil-design-eng/SKILL.md)\n- find-animation-opportunities: Search a codebase or UI for places that don't animate but should, and reject everything that shouldn't. Read-only; it proposes motion with exact values, it does not implement it. Use when the user asks \"what could be animated here?\" or wants to \"make this feel more alive\". For fixing existing animations, use improve-animations or review-animations instead. (file: r8/find-animation-opportunities/SKILL.md)\n- full-output-enforcement: Overrides default LLM truncation behavior. Enforces complete code generation, bans placeholder patterns, and handles token-limit splits cleanly. Apply to any task requiring exhaustive, unabridged output. (file: r8/full-output-enforcement/SKILL.md)\n- impeccable: Use when the user wants to design, redesign, shape, critique, audit, polish, clarify, distill, harden, optimize, adapt, animate, colorize, extract, or otherwise improve a frontend interface. Covers websites, landing pages, dashboards, product UI, app shells, components, forms, settings, onboarding, and empty states. Handles UX review, visual hierarchy, information architecture, cognitive load, accessibility, performance, responsive behavior, theming, anti-patterns, typography, fonts, spacing, layout, alig (file: r8/impeccable/SKILL.md)\n- improve-animations: Survey a codebase's animation and motion code as a senior motion advisor, then produce a prioritized audit and self-contained implementation plans for other agents (or cheaper models) to execute. Read-only on source code — it plans improvements, it does not apply them. Use when the user asks to \"improve the animations\", \"audit the motion\", \"make this app feel better\", or wants a roadmap of animation fixes rather than a review of a single diff. (file: r8/improve-animations/SKILL.md)\n- mobile-native: Make a web app feel native on a phone — the small CSS and meta-tag fixes that separate \"a website in a browser\" from something that feels installed. Covers sticky hover states, tap highlight flashes, the 100vh bug, inputs that zoom the page, laggy taps, pull-to-refresh hijacking scroll, content under the notch, long-press selecting button text, carousels that scroll the wrong way, mismatched status bars, and the rule that you test on real hardware. Use when a web app is being built for or reviewed on mobi (file: r8/mobile-native/SKILL.md)\n- pick-ui-library: Pick the right library for a given frontend task from a curated, opinionated list — numbers, OTP inputs, charts, command menus, virtualization, drag and drop, toasts, state, styling, and more. Only runs when explicitly invoked; it does not trigger on its own. (file: r8/pick-ui-library/SKILL.md)\n- prototype: Build multiple genuinely different versions of a UI piece you describe, rendered behind a visual picker so you can flip through them live and promote the one that feels right. Only runs when explicitly invoked; it does not trigger on its own. (file: r8/prototype/SKILL.md)\n- review-animations: Reviews animation and motion code against a high craft bar derived from Emil Kowalski's design engineering philosophy. Default to flagging; approval is earned. (file: r8/review-animations/SKILL.md)\n- agent-tools: Run 150+ AI apps via inference.sh CLI - image generation, video creation, LLMs, search, 3D, Twitter automation. Models: FLUX, Veo, Gemini, Grok, Claude, Seedance, OmniHuman, Tavily, Exa, OpenRouter, and many more. Use when running AI apps, generating images/videos, calling LLMs, web search, or automating Twitter. Triggers: inference.sh, infsh, ai model, run ai, serverless ai, ai api, flux, veo, claude api, image generation, video generation, openrouter, tavily, exa search, twitter api, grok (file: r1/agent-tools/SKILL.md)\n- agents-sdk: Build, debug, or review Cloudflare Agents SDK applications using the agents package. (file: r1/agents-sdk/SKILL.md)\n- approved-server-access-bundle: Use when given an approved YS Insight server access GitHub issue and asked to generate an Aliyun Web SSH upload bundle, public key file, concise execution steps, or completion reply. (file: r1/approved-server-access-bundle/SKILL.md)\n- babysit-pr: Monitor a pull request through review and CI. Use when the user asks to monitor, watch, or babysit a PR. (file: r0/babysit-pr/SKILL.md)\n- basin: Build and troubleshoot Cloudflare Basin analytics workflows with Basin Pipelines, Basin Catalog, and Basin SQL. Use for streaming data into R2 Iceberg tables, managing catalogs, or querying those tables; also use for requests using the former Data Platform, Pipelines, R2 Data Catalog, or R2 SQL names. (file: r1/basin/SKILL.md)\n- cf-cli: Cloudflare's official cf CLI (beta) for the public Cloudflare API and Workers projects. Use for finding and running Cloudflare commands from the terminal — sign-in and profiles, cf cli search, cf schema, cf deploy/dev/build, resource commands for DNS, zones, D1, KV, and R2 — and for choosing between cf and Wrangler in a project. (file: r1/cf-cli/SKILL.md)\n- clipsmith-audio-transcript: Convert a local audio file to text by running an explicit local transcription command. Use after extracting audio from video. (file: r0/clipsmith-audio-transcript/SKILL.md)\n- clipsmith-capture: Capture a URL or local media input into a portable Clipsmith bundle. Routes to platform-specific Clipsmith skills and validates the resulting bundle. (file: r0/clipsmith-capture/SKILL.md)\n- clipsmith-ocr: Run native macOS image OCR through Vision.framework via pyobjc bridge (same engine family used by Preview/Live Text), with Chinese+English recognition defaults and deterministic CLI execution. Use when you need to extract text from local images on macOS — including screenshots, photos, or scanned documents — and want output to stdout or a text file. Also known as image-to-text or read-text-from-image. (file: r0/clipsmith-ocr/SKILL.md)\n- clipsmith-video-audio: Extract an audio sidecar from a local video file with ffmpeg. Use when you need the voice/audio track from a video before transcription. (file: r0/clipsmith-video-audio/SKILL.md)\n- clipsmith-video-snapshot: Extract timestamped image snapshots from a local video with ffmpeg. Use clipsmith-ocr separately when OCR is needed for the snapshots. (file: r0/clipsmith-video-snapshot/SKILL.md)\n- clipsmith-web: Capture a generic web article into a Clipsmith bundle using browser or fetch-based extraction available to the agent. (file: r0/clipsmith-web/SKILL.md)\n- clipsmith-wechat: Browser-only automation skill to export a WeChat public account article into a local folder, including article.mhtml, article.md, and all embedded images. (file: r0/clipsmith-wechat/SKILL.md)\n- clipsmith-x: Download X (Twitter) post assets — text, images, video — to a local folder using browser automation with manual-login session reuse. Use when downloading an X post, saving post images, exporting post content, or archiving a tweet. Trigger phrases: \"download x post\", \"save tweet\", \"x post images\", \"x video download\", \"twitter post assets\". (file: r0/clipsmith-x/SKILL.md)\n- clipsmith-xhs: Download XiaoHongShu (XHS / xiaohongshu) post assets — images, video, text metadata — to a local folder using browser automation with manual-login session reuse. Use when downloading a XHS post, saving post images, exporting post content, or archiving a note. Trigger phrases: \"download xhs\", \"xiaohongshu post\", \"xhs images\", \"save post\", \"xhs note\", \"xiaohongshu download\". (file: r0/clipsmith-xhs/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare: Discover and choose Cloudflare products for apps, APIs, AI agents, storage, networking, and security. Use for architecture and product selection, including when the user describes a need without naming a Cloudflare product; then find the relevant skill or documentation. (file: r1/cloudflare/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare-email-service: Implement or troubleshoot Cloudflare Email Sending and Email Routing integrations and their delivery configuration. (file: r1/cloudflare-email-service/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare-one: Design, configure, troubleshoot, or review Cloudflare One Zero Trust and SASE deployments. Use cloudflare-one-migrations for migration planning from other vendors. (file: r1/cloudflare-one/SKILL.md)\n- cloudflare-one-migrations: Assess and plan migrations from existing VPN, SWG, or SASE platforms to Cloudflare One, including policy mapping, parity gaps, and rollout. (file: r1/cloudflare-one-migrations/SKILL.md)\n- council: Trigger this if the user says 'council'. (file: r0/council/SKILL.md)\n- durable-objects: Build, debug, or review Cloudflare Durable Objects code for persistent state and coordination. (file: r1/durable-objects/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-3d-transforms: Add or edit 3D transforms — depth, perspective, z-axis rotation, tilt, isometric, and parallax looks that flat 2D rotation cannot express. Use whenever a request reaches into the third dimension — \"make it 3D\", \"add depth/perspective\", \"tilt/rotate it back\", \"z-plane\", \"isometric\", \"flip it like a card\", \"parallax\", \"vanishing point\", \"receding into the distance\", \"make it pop out\". (file: r3/figma-3d-transforms/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-code-connect: Creates and maintains Figma Code Connect template files that map Figma components to code snippets. Use when the user mentions Code Connect, Figma component mapping, design-to-code translation, or asks to create/update .figma.ts or .figma.js files. (file: r3/figma-code-connect/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-code-workflows: Import or upload local HTML or project files into Figma, choose editable Design frames, runnable Code Layers or standalone Code (also known as Make), and repair imported previews. Use only when the user explicitly names editable Design layers, a runnable Code Layer, or a standalone Code/Make file, or supplies a Figma file key/link as the import destination. Generic code or code-component requests need output clarification before using this workflow. (file: r3/figma-code-workflows/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-create-new-file: Always use this skill when the user wants to create a new Figma Design, FigJam, or Slides file. You MUST invoke this skill BEFORE every `create_new_file` tool call. (file: r3/figma-create-new-file/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-design-to-code: **MANDATORY prerequisite** — you MUST invoke this skill BEFORE calling the `get_design_context` Figma MCP tool. You MUST trigger this skill whenever the user wants to implement, build, port, or code up a Figma design as code. Example prompts (not exhaustive) are 'implement this Figma design', 'build this screen from Figma', 'turn this Figma into code', 'design to code'. This skill provides critical instructions and steps to the agent on how to correctly implement Figma designs in code and must NOT be skip (file: r3/figma-design-to-code/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-generate-design: Use this skill alongside figma-use when the task involves translating an application page, view, or multi-section layout into Figma. Triggers: 'write to Figma', 'create in Figma from code', 'push page to Figma', 'take this app/page and build it in Figma', 'create a screen', 'build a landing page in Figma', 'update the Figma screen to match code', 'convert this modal/dialog/drawer/panel to Figma'. This is the preferred workflow skill whenever the user wants to build or update a full page, modal, dialog, dr (file: r3/figma-generate-design/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-generate-diagram: MANDATORY prerequisite — load this skill BEFORE every `generate_diagram` tool call. NEVER call `generate_diagram` directly without loading this skill first. Trigger whenever the user asks to create, generate, draw, render, sketch, or build a diagram — flowchart, architecture diagram, sequence diagram, ERD or entity-relationship diagram, state diagram or state machine, gantt chart, or timeline. Also trigger when the user mentions Mermaid syntax or wants a system architecture, decision tree, dependency graph, (file: r3/figma-generate-diagram/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-generate-library: Build or update a professional-grade design system in Figma from a codebase. Use when the user wants to create variables/tokens, build component libraries, create individual components with proper variant sets and variable bindings, set up theming (light/dark modes), document foundations, or reconcile gaps between code and Figma. Also use when the user asks to create or generate any component in Figma — even a single one — since components require proper variable foundations, variant states, and design toke (file: r3/figma-generate-library/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-generative-plugins: **MANDATORY prerequisite** — load this skill before calling `create_generative_plugin` or `update_generative_plugin`. Use when the user asks to create, author, change, fix, or extend a reusable generative Figma plugin. (file: r3/figma-generative-plugins/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-implement-motion: Translates Figma motion and animations into production-ready application code. Use when implementing animation/motion from a Figma design — user mentions \"implement this motion\", \"add animation from Figma\", \"animate this component\", provides a Figma URL whose node is animated, or when `get_design_context` returns motion data or instructs you to call `get_motion_context`. (file: r3/figma-implement-motion/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-shaders: **MANDATORY prerequisite** — load this skill before calling `create_shader` or `update_shader`. Use when the user asks to create, author, change, fix, or iterate on a shader effect, shader fill, custom effect, custom fill, or procedural shader in Figma. (file: r3/figma-shaders/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-spec: This skill is required to create, read, edit, review, or comment on Figma specs. You must read it when working on a PRD, brief, RFC or any kind of document in either a standalone Figma spec or a Figma spec embedded in a Figma Design or Figjam file. (file: r3/figma-spec/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-swiftui: SwiftUI ↔ Figma translation. Use whenever the user mentions Swift, SwiftUI, iOS, iPhone, or iPad — in EITHER direction — translating a Figma design into SwiftUI (design → code), or pushing SwiftUI views / screens / tokens back into a Figma file (code → design). Triggers on phrases like 'implement this Figma design in SwiftUI', 'build this screen in Swift', 'push this SwiftUI view to Figma', 'mirror my Swift code in a Figma file', or whenever a Figma URL appears alongside `.swift` files / an `.xcodeproj`. Ro (file: r3/figma-swiftui/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-use: **MANDATORY prerequisite** — you MUST invoke this skill BEFORE every `use_figma` tool call. NEVER call `use_figma` directly without loading this skill first. Skipping it causes common, hard-to-debug failures. Trigger whenever the user wants to perform a write action or a unique read action that requires JavaScript execution in the Figma file context — e.g. create/edit/delete nodes, set up variables or tokens, build components and variants, modify auto-layout or fills, bind variables to properties, or insp (file: r3/figma-use/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-use-figjam: This skill helps agents use Figma's use_figma MCP tool in the FigJam context. Can be used alongside figma-use which has foundational context for using the use_figma tool. (file: r3/figma-use-figjam/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-use-motion: Motion / animation context for the `use_figma` MCP tool — animating Figma nodes via manual keyframes, animation styles, easing, and timeline duration. Load alongside figma-use whenever a task involves adding, editing, or inspecting animation on a node. Also covers working with custom animation styles. (file: r3/figma-use-motion/SKILL.md)\n- figma:figma-use-slides: This skill helps agents use Figma's use_figma MCP tool in the Slides context. Can be used alongside figma-use which has foundational context for using the use_figma tool. (file: r3/figma-use-slides/SKILL.md)\n- find-skills: Helps users discover and install agent skills when they ask questions like \"how do I do X\", \"find a skill for X\", \"is there a skill that can...\", or express interest in extending capabilities. This skill should be used when the user is looking for functionality that might exist as an installable skill. (file: r1/find-skills/SKILL.md)\n- frontend-design: Guidance for distinctive, intentional visual design when building new UI or reshaping an existing one. Helps with aesthetic direction, typography, and making choices that don't read as templated defaults. (file: r0/frontend-design/SKILL.md)\n- full-send: Trigger this if the user says 'full send'. (file: r0/full-send/SKILL.md)\n- full-stop: Trigger this if the user says 'full stop', even as a closing \"Full stop.\" that reads like emphasis. (file: r0/full-stop/SKILL.md)\n- gateway-workers: Build, debug, or review Cloudflare Workers invoked by the Forward action in Gateway HTTP policies, including the GatewayMiddleware entrypoint, Gateway types, context, and continuation. (file: r1/gateway-workers/SKILL.md)\n- inbox-peek: Use when reviewing the oldest inbox item. (file: r1/inbox-peek/SKILL.md)\n- k2: Build and troubleshoot Cloudflare K2 or K2 Streams durable logs. Use for stream setup, producing from Workers or HTTP, configuring retention and inputs, and consuming through subscriptions. (file: r1/k2/SKILL.md)\n- labali-blog-writing-style: Create, rewrite, or refine personal blog posts in Kingson Wu's established voice for Chinese technical essays, workplace reflections, and reading notes. Use when the task is to draft a new article, revise an existing post, or make AI-written content sound like the author's earlier blog voice while preserving core meaning and improving structure, hierarchy, and readability. Trigger phrases: \"write a blog post\", \"blog writing\", \"rewrite article\", \"refine post\", \"写博客\", \"改文章\", \"写文章\". (file: r1/labali-blog-writing-style/SKILL.md)\n- labali-git-auto-commit-rewrite: Generate a clear conventional commit message from current git changes, stage all files, commit locally, and run clean_commit.sh to normalize the result. Use when users want to commit, stage and commit, auto-commit, or generate a commit message for current changes. Trigger phrases: \"commit changes\", \"git commit\", \"commit all\", \"commit message\", \"conventional commit\". (file: r1/labali-git-auto-commit-rewrite/SKILL.md)\n- labali-spotify-publish-episode: Publish podcast episodes on Spotify for Creators using browser-only semantic automation with manual-login session reuse. Use when asked to publish, upload, release, or schedule a podcast episode on Spotify for Creators (creators.spotify.com). Trigger phrases: \"publish episode\", \"upload podcast\", \"release episode\", \"schedule podcast\", \"spotify creator\". (file: r1/labali-spotify-publish-episode/SKILL.md)\n- mattpocock-skills:code-review: Review the changes since a fixed point (commit, branch, tag, or merge-base) along two axes — Standards (does the code follow this repo's documented coding standards?) and Spec (does the code match what the originating issue/PRD asked for?). Runs both reviews in parallel sub-agents and reports them side by side. Use when the user wants to review a branch, a PR, work-in-progress changes, or asks to \"review since X\". (file: r1/code-review/SKILL.md)\n- mattpocock-skills:codebase-design: Shared vocabulary for designing deep modules. Use when the user wants to design or improve a module's interface, find deepening opportunities, decide where a seam goes, make code more testable or AI-navigable, or when another skill needs the deep-module vocabulary. (file: r1/codebase-design/SKILL.md)\n- mattpocock-skills:diagnosing-bugs: Diagnosis loop for hard bugs and performance regressions. Use when the user says \"diagnose\"/\"debug this\", or reports something broken/throwing/failing/slow. (file: r1/diagnosing-bugs/SKILL.md)\n- mattpocock-skills:domain-modeling: Build and sharpen a project's domain model. Use when the user wants to pin down domain terminology or a ubiquitous language, record an architectural decision, or when another skill needs to maintain the domain model. (file: r1/domain-modeling/SKILL.md)\n- mattpocock-skills:git-guardrails-claude-code: Set up Claude Code hooks to block dangerous git commands (push, reset --hard, clean, branch -D, etc.) before they execute. Use when user wants to prevent destructive git operations, add git safety hooks, or block git push/reset in Claude Code. (file: r1/git-guardrails-claude-code/SKILL.md)\n- mattpocock-skills:grilling: Grill the user relentlessly about a plan, decision, or idea. Use when the user wants to stress-test their thinking, or uses any 'grill' trigger phrases. (file: r1/grilling/SKILL.md)\n- mattpocock-skills:research: Investigate a question against high-trust primary sources and capture the findings as a Markdown file in the repo. Use when the user wants a topic researched, docs or API facts gathered, or reading legwork delegated to a background agent. (file: r1/research/SKILL.md)\n- mattpocock-skills:resolving-merge-conflicts: Use when you need to resolve an in-progress git merge/rebase conflict. (file: r1/resolving-merge-conflicts/SKILL.md)\n- mattpocock-skills:setup-pre-commit: Set up Husky pre-commit hooks with lint-staged (Prettier), type checking, and tests in the current repo. Use when user wants to add pre-commit hooks, set up Husky, configure lint-staged, or add commit-time formatting/typechecking/testing. (file: r1/setup-pre-commit/SKILL.md)\n- mattpocock-skills:tdd: Test-driven development. Use when the user wants to build features or fix bugs test-first, mentions \"red-green-refactor\", or wants integration tests. (file: r1/tdd/SKILL.md)\n- net-auto-switch: Operate and diagnose net-auto-switch — the macOS daemon that auto-switches the Clash Verge proxy node (and WiFi) by measured network quality. Use when the user asks what proxy node they're on, why the proxy is slow, which operator/cloud is behind a node or domain, what their machine is connecting to right now, or to check / start / stop / update the background service. 诊断代理:当前走哪个节点、某节点或域名属于哪个运营商/云、本机此刻在访问哪些域名、后台服务是否在跑。Not for editing Clash subscriptions or the Clash config itself. (file: r1/net-auto-switch/SKILL.md)\n- nextjs-on-cloudflare: Build, migrate, and deploy Next.js apps on Cloudflare Workers with vinext. Use when starting a Next.js project on Cloudflare, moving an existing app to Workers, choosing between vinext and OpenNext, or setting up vinext for Workers. For setup, migration, or deployment, install vinext's upstream skills with `npx skills add cloudflare/vinext` if missing, then read and follow the applicable skill and docs. (file: r1/nextjs-on-cloudflare/SKILL.md)\n- pages:maintain-space: Reconcile new evidence into existing ChatGPT Pages or a Space when the user requests upkeep. For a direct text correction, use write-page. Do not use for ordinary chat drafts, writing, or advice, or treat a selected Page as a request to save. Schedule future upkeep only when requested. (file: r4/maintain-space/SKILL.md)\n- pages:manage-schedules: Review a ChatGPT Space page and its schedules, recommend useful recurring work, and create, update, or remove scheduled automations. (file: r4/manage-schedules/SKILL.md)\n- pages:organize-space: Organize existing ChatGPT Pages and Space structure when the user requests changes to that structure. Do not use for ordinary chat drafts or advice; a selected Page alone does not authorize reorganization. A narrow text edit does not need this workflow. (file: r4/organize-space/SKILL.md)\n- pages:write-page: Create or edit requested Page/Space content, or use for prose you have already decided to save as a standalone Markdown file, only if the user did not explicitly request a Markdown file. Honor established formats, destinations, and repository documentation. A selected Page alone does not authorize a write. (file: r4/write-page/SKILL.md)\n- paseo: Paseo reference for managing hosts, projects, workspaces, workspace scripts, agents, schedules, and heartbeats. (file: r1/paseo/SKILL.md)\n- paseo-advisor: Spin up a single agent as an advisor — second opinion on the current task. Use when the user says \"advisor\", \"second opinion\", \"what does X think\", or wants an outside take without delegating the work itself. (file: r1/paseo-advisor/SKILL.md)\n- paseo-committee: Form a committee of two high-reasoning agents to step back, do root cause analysis, and produce a plan. Use when stuck, looping, tunnel-visioning, or facing a hard planning problem. (file: r1/paseo-committee/SKILL.md)\n- paseo-handoff: Hand off the current task to another agent with full context. Use when the user says \"handoff\", \"hand off\", \"hand this to\", or wants to pass work to another agent. (file: r1/paseo-handoff/SKILL.md)\n- paseo-help: Answer questions about the Paseo product and app, including setup, configuration, connectivity, providers, workspaces, updates, logs, and troubleshooting. Use when a user inside Paseo asks how Paseo works, how to configure it, or why something is broken; use the paseo skill instead to operate agents and workspaces through MCP or the CLI. (file: r1/paseo-help/SKILL.md)\n- paseo-plugin: Build and manage trusted local Paseo plugins. Use when the user asks to create, edit, install, reload, enable, disable, remove, or troubleshoot a Paseo plugin; add lifecycle hooks; transform agent configuration, environment, MCP servers, or workspace creation; automate permissions or turn follow-ups; add a screen, sidebar header or footer item, workspace panel, or file panel; add Command Center items or slash commands; add composer pills or attachment sources; transform, render, or append agent timeline i (file: r1/paseo-plugin/SKILL.md)\n- playwright-trace: Inspect Playwright trace files from the command line — list actions, view requests, console, errors, snapshots and screenshots. (file: r0/clipsmith-web/node_modules/playwright-core/lib/tools/trace/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-management:plugin-management: Discover and suggest relevant plugins, inspect app permissions and dependencies, and manage plugin connections or removal. Use when the user asks about plugins or when a task would materially benefit from an external app, account, service, or data source that available tools cannot access. (file: r5/plugin-management/0.1.0/skills/plugin-management/SKILL.md)\n- sandbox-migrate-to-next: Migrate Cloudflare Sandbox apps from stable @cloudflare/sandbox to @cloudflare/sandbox@next (SDK 1.0 preview). Use sandbox-next for apps already on the preview. (file: r1/sandbox-migrate-to-next/SKILL.md)\n- sandbox-next: Build or maintain Cloudflare Sandbox apps on @cloudflare/sandbox@next (SDK 1.0 preview). Use sandbox-migrate-to-next when porting a stable app. (file: r1/sandbox-next/SKILL.md)\n- sandbox-stable: Build or maintain Cloudflare Sandbox apps on the stable @cloudflare/sandbox package. Use sandbox-next for preview apps and sandbox-migrate-to-next for stable-to-preview migrations. (file: r1/sandbox-stable/SKILL.md)\n- sites:sites: Use Sites when the user wants a website built for them (including a landing page, portfolio, dashboard, portal, tracker, hub, game, event registration, or tool), requests changes to a website already built with Sites, wants to publish a website with Sites, or wants to manage Sites hosting. (file: r6/sites/SKILL.md)\n- sites:sites-remix: Remix a Site built with the Sites plugin into a new Site. Use when the user has identified the source Site and asks to remix it. (file: r6/sites-remix/SKILL.md)\n- social-post-manager: Use when handling the reviewed inbox item or saving discussion notes. (file: r1/social-post-manager/SKILL.md)\n- sol-loop: Use when the user says \"sol loop\". (file: r0/sol-loop/SKILL.md)\n- superpowers:brainstorming: You MUST use this before any creative work - creating features, building components, adding functionality, or modifying behavior. Explores user intent, requirements and design before implementation. (file: r1/superpowers/brainstorming/SKILL.md)\n- superpowers:dispatching-parallel-agents: Use when facing 2+ independent tasks that can be worked on without shared state or sequential dependencies (file: r1/superpowers/dispatching-parallel-agents/SKILL.md)\n- superpowers:executing-plans: Use when you have a written implementation plan to execute in a separate session with review checkpoints (file: r1/superpowers/executing-plans/SKILL.md)\n- superpowers:finishing-a-development-branch: Use when implementation is complete, all tests pass, and you need to decide how to integrate the work - guides completion of development work by presenting structured options for merge, PR, or cleanup (file: r1/superpowers/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md)\n- superpowers:subagent-driven-development: Use when executing implementation plans with independent tasks in the current session (file: r1/superpowers/subagent-driven-development/SKILL.md)\n- superpowers:systematic-debugging: Use when encountering any bug, test failure, or unexpected behavior, before proposing fixes (file: r1/superpowers/systematic-debugging/SKILL.md)\n- superpowers:test-driven-development: Use when implementing any feature or bugfix, before writing implementation code (file: r1/superpowers/test-driven-development/SKILL.md)\n- superpowers:using-git-worktrees: Use when starting feature work that needs isolation from current workspace or before executing implementation plans - creates isolated git worktrees with smart directory selection and safety verification (file: r1/superpowers/using-git-worktrees/SKILL.md)\n- superpowers:using-superpowers: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring Skill tool invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions (file: r1/superpowers/using-superpowers/SKILL.md)\n- superpowers:verification-before-completion: Use when about to claim work is complete, fixed, or passing, before committing or creating PRs - requires running verification commands and confirming output before making any success claims; evidence before assertions always (file: r1/superpowers/verification-before-completion/SKILL.md)\n- superpowers:writing-plans: Use when you have a spec or requirements for a multi-step task, before touching code (file: r1/superpowers/writing-plans/SKILL.md)\n- superpowers:writing-skills: Use when creating new skills, editing existing skills, or verifying skills work before deployment (file: r1/superpowers/writing-skills/SKILL.md)\n- turnstile-spin: Set up, repair, or migrate to Cloudflare Turnstile bot verification in an existing frontend and backend, including server-side Siteverify. (file: r1/turnstile-spin/SKILL.md)\n- visualize:visualize: Create visualizations and interactive tools directly in conversation. Proactively use to show how something works; explore 'what happens when', 'what changes', or 'help me understand'; compare or inspect; create simulations, maps, charts, graphs, and mockups. Use standard tools for static scientific figures. (file: r5/visualize/1.0.89/skills/visualize/SKILL.md)\n- web-perf: Audit, diagnose, or optimize website loading and interaction performance, Core Web Vitals, and Lighthouse performance scores. (file: r1/web-perf/SKILL.md)\n- work-pets:create-pet: Create, repair, validate, preview, upload, or activate an animated v2 pet in ChatGPT Work mode from a character idea, brand cue, or reference image. Use for new ChatGPT Pets, custom mascots, non-pixel styles, and unattached sprite sheets requiring nine animation states plus sixteen look directions. Preserve Library artifacts and Pets MCP lifecycle behavior. Exclude real pets, standalone images, and other-app pets; clarify before invoking. (file: r7/create-pet/SKILL.md)\n- work-pets:pets: List, inspect, select, download, or delete animated pets in ChatGPT Work mode. Use for an explicit pet in ChatGPT or established ChatGPT Pets context. Exclude real-world and other-app pets; clarify before invoking. (file: r7/pets/SKILL.md)\n- work-pets:update-pet: Inspect, validate, preview, repair, or update a custom pet in ChatGPT Work mode, including its name, description, or sprite sheet. Use for a pet in ChatGPT or established ChatGPT Pets context. Exclude real-world and other-app pets; clarify before invoking. (file: r7/update-pet/SKILL.md)\n- workers-best-practices: Cloudflare Workers best practices for production applications. Use when writing, reviewing, or configuring Workers. (file: r1/workers-best-practices/SKILL.md)\n- workers-profiling: Profile or debug CPU usage and memory allocation in deployed Cloudflare Workers and Durable Objects. Then use this information to optimize your code. (file: r1/workers-profiling/SKILL.md)\n- wrangler: Run or troubleshoot Wrangler CLI commands in projects that have a wrangler.jsonc, wrangler.json, or wrangler.toml file, including local development, Previews, deployment, and resource management. For new projects and projects without a Wrangler configuration file, use the cf CLI instead. (file: r1/wrangler/SKILL.md)\n- ysinsight-context: Explicit-only shortcut for searching YS Insight / 悦数 internal company and team documentation. Trigger only when the user invokes the ysinsight-context shortcut directly, such as $ysinsight-context, or says to use the ysinsight-context shortcut to search internal docs. Do not trigger for installing, configuring, releasing, debugging, or discussing the ysinsight-context MCP/package/server. (file: r1/ysinsight-context/SKILL.md)\n- ysinsight-context-refresh: Explicit-only shortcut for refreshing YS Insight / 悦数 internal documentation context. Trigger only when the user names ysinsight-context-refresh exactly or explicitly asks to refresh/sync/update YS Insight internal docs. Do not trigger when the user only mentions ysinsight-context MCP/package/server, normal documentation searches, or public docs. (file: r1/ysinsight-context-refresh/SKILL.md)\n- ysinsight-skills:ysinsight-code-pulse: Score the current local git repo against code-pulse health rules (0-100) entirely locally — the local agent acts as the LLM (no API key, no DashScope) and reads files in the current directory (no GitHub token required). Produces a full health report with per-rule breakdown and N/A redistribution. GitHub-only rules (dependabot / workflow runs / description) are filled best-effort via `gh` if authenticated, else marked N/A. Use when you want this repo's code-pulse health score, to self-check before pushing,  (file: r0/ysinsight-code-pulse/SKILL.md)\n- ysinsight-skills:ysinsight-code-pulse-fix: Fix half of the code-pulse pair — run ysinsight-code-pulse to get the full 0-100 score + per-rule gap report, then remediate gaps (CLAUDE.md, docs/, .gitignore, language-specific quality gate with lint/format/type/SAST/dep/test/coverage, git hooks, CI, GitHub-side settings). Use when raising a repo's code-pulse health score or conforming to org standards. (file: r0/ysinsight-code-pulse-fix/SKILL.md)\n- ysinsight-skills:ysinsight-github-security-fix: Scrape GitHub security findings (Dependabot, CodeQL, Code Scanning, AI Findings) via browser automation and assess/fix them. Use when user asks to check, review, or fix security vulnerabilities, dependency alerts, or code quality issues in a GitHub repo. (file: r0/ysinsight-github-security-fix/SKILL.md)\n\u003c/skills_instructions\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003cpermissions instructions\u003e\nFilesystem sandboxing defines which files can be read or written. `sandbox_mode` is `danger-full-access`: No filesystem sandboxing - all commands are permitted. Network access is enabled.\nApproval policy is currently never. Do not provide the `sandbox_permissions` for any reason, commands will be rejected.\n\u003c/permissions instructions\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003ccollaboration_mode\u003e# Collaboration Mode: Default\n\nYou are now in Default mode. Any previous instructions for other modes (e.g. Plan mode) are no longer active.\n\nYour active mode changes only when new developer instructions with a different `\u003ccollaboration_mode\u003e...\u003c/collaboration_mode\u003e` change it; user requests or tool descriptions do not change mode by themselves. Known mode names are Default and Plan.\n\n## request_user_input availability\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only when it is listed in the available tools for this turn.\n\nIn Default mode, strongly prefer making reasonable assumptions and executing the user's request rather than stopping to ask questions.\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only for optional questions where the answer would materially improve the quality of the work.\n\nIf `request_user_input` returns no answers, continue with best judgment instead of asking again or treating the turn as blocked.\n\nNever use the `request_user_input` tool for permission requests or permission-related escalations.\n\u003c/collaboration_mode\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003ctools\u003e\nDeferred tool namespaces:\n- mcp__alcove\n- mcp__cloudflare\n- mcp__codegraph: # Codegraph — available (per-project; pass projectPath)\n- mcp__context7: Use this server to fetch current documentation whenever the user asks about a library, framework, SDK, API, CLI tool, or cloud service — even well-known ones like React, Next.js, Prisma, Express, Tailwind, Django, or Spring Boot. This includes API...\n- mcp__english_pilot\n- mcp__t3_code\n- mcp__ysinsight_context: Use ysinsight-context only when the user clearly intends to search YS Insight / 悦数 company, team, or internal documentation.\n\u003c/tools\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a20698-e310-7550-b69d-7588657b7fe6","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["host_skills.instructions","permissions.instructions","collaboration_mode.instructions","tools.deferred_namespaces"],"content_item_metadata":[{"provenance":{"type":"skills"}},{"provenance":{"hook":false,"type":"harness"}},{"provenance":{"hook":false,"type":"harness"}},{"provenance":{"namespace":"tool_search","type":"tool"}}],"turn_id":"01a20696-1213-7100-8faa-48c44a76bc9e"},"role":"developer","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003cmulti_agent_role\u003eYou are `/root`, the primary agent in a team of agents collaborating to fulfill the user's goals.\n\nAt the start of your turn, you are the active agent. Your final answer will be the response to the user\nAll agents in the team have access to the same set of tools.\n\nAll agents share the same workspace, filesystem, and tool state, meaning any changes will be immediately reflected in your local state.\n\nWith `fork_turns=\"all\"` (the default), the child can see the parent’s conversation up to the fork. With `\"none\"`, it starts without that conversation. In either case, the child receives its own role and task.\n\nYou will receive messages in the commentary channel in the form:\n```\nMessage Type: MESSAGE | FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: \u003crecipient\u003e\nSender: \u003cauthor\u003e\nPayload:\n\u003cpayload text\u003e\n```\nThey will be preceded by a header of the form `assistant:\u003csender\u003e to=/root`\nNote that collaboration tools cannot be called from inside `functions.exec`. Call `spawn_agent`, `send_message`, `followup_task`, `wait_agent`, `interrupt_agent`, and `list_agents` only as direct tool calls using the recipient shown in their tool definitions, such as `to=functions.collaboration.spawn_agent`, since they are intentionally absent from the `functions.exec` `tools.*` namespace. Available tools in `functions.exec` are explicitly described with a `tools` namespace in the developer message.\n\nAll agents share the same directory. In detail:\n- All agents have access to the same container and filesystem as you.\n- All agents use the same current working directory.\n- As a result, edits made by one agent are immediately visible to all other agents.\n\nWhen calling `wait_agent`, prefer longer waits (minutes) to avoid busy polling.\n\nThere are 4 available concurrency slots, meaning that up to 4 agents can be active at once, including you.\n\nFull-history forks (`fork_turns` omitted or `\"all\"`) inherit the parent model and reasoning effort and do not accept overrides. Only set `model` or `reasoning_effort` when explicitly requested by the user, applicable `AGENTS.md` instructions, or skill instructions; when doing so, set `fork_turns` to `\"none\"`.\u003c/multi_agent_role\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a20698-e310-7550-b69d-7597f49493aa","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["multi_agent.role_instructions"],"content_item_metadata":[{"provenance":{"namespace":"collaboration","type":"tool"}}],"turn_id":"01a20696-1213-7100-8faa-48c44a76bc9e"},"role":"developer","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003cmulti_agent_mode\u003eAny earlier instruction enabling proactive multi-agent delegation no longer applies. Do not spawn sub-agents unless the user or applicable AGENTS.md/skill instructions explicitly ask for sub-agents, delegation, or parallel agent work.\u003c/multi_agent_mode\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a20698-e310-7550-b69d-75a1fb2b7d69","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["multi_agent.mode_instructions"],"content_item_metadata":[{"provenance":{"namespace":"collaboration","type":"tool"}}],"turn_id":"01a20696-1213-7100-8faa-48c44a76bc9e"},"role":"developer","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"# AGENTS.md instructions for /Users/kingsonwu/programming/OctopusGarage/mesh-talk\n\n\u003cINSTRUCTIONS\u003e\n\u003c!-- CODEGRAPH_START --\u003e\n## CodeGraph\n\nIn repositories indexed by CodeGraph (a `.codegraph/` directory exists at the repo root), reach for it BEFORE grep/find or reading files when you need to understand or locate code:\n\n- **MCP tool** (when available): `codegraph_explore` answers most code questions in one call — the relevant symbols' verbatim source plus the call paths between them, including dynamic-dispatch hops grep can't follow. Name a file or symbol in the query to read its current line-numbered source. If it's listed but deferred, load it by name via tool search.\n- **Shell** (always works): `codegraph explore \"\u003csymbol names or question\u003e\"` prints the same output.\n\nIf there is no `.codegraph/` directory, skip CodeGraph entirely — indexing is the user's decision.\n\u003c!-- CODEGRAPH_END --\u003e\n\n\u003c!-- ENGLISH_PILOT_GUIDANCE_START --\u003e\n## EnglishPilot\n\nDo not derail the main task. When the user prompt is allowed, complete the requested work first.\nIf the prompt contains any Chinese fragment, awkward English, or an obvious everyday phrasing improvement, add one English note at the end of the final response when allowed and teachable.\nIn force/high intensity, treat this as required unless there is genuinely no useful improvement.\n\nWhen the english-pilot MCP server is available, use `english_coaching_context` to get the configured note depth and response guidance before writing the final answer.\nUse `english_rewrite_text` and `english_pronounce_text` for structured help when useful.\n\nIf MCP is unavailable, prefer this fallback format:\nUse a Rich English Note by default: 5-8 useful lines, brief enough for a final response but rich enough for study.\nUse this shape:\nEnglish note:\nOriginal: \"fix this problem\"\nBetter: \"fix this issue\" / \"resolve this startup issue\"\nWhy: In engineering conversations, \"issue\" is more precise than \"problem\" when discussing bugs, failures, tickets, or startup errors.\nUseful patterns:\n- \"The MCP client failed during the startup handshake.\"\n- \"This issue belongs at the config boundary, not the runtime path.\"\nCollocations: startup issue, handshake failure, config mismatch, reproduce the issue, verify the fix\nCommon mistake: Use \"problem\" for general trouble; use \"issue\" for bugs, tickets, incidents, and engineering discussions.\nIPA: issue /ˈɪʃuː/ or /ˈɪsjuː/\n\nWhen the user asks for a recap, lesson, speech, or review based on their history, use `english_learning_brief`, `english_input_history`, or `english_notes_history` before answering.\nDo not add an English note for prompts blocked by the EnglishPilot hook.\n\u003c!-- ENGLISH_PILOT_GUIDANCE_END --\u003e\n\u003c!-- MESH_TALK_AGENT_ACCESS_START --\u003e\nMesh Talk local API: \"http://127.0.0.1:57987/v1/help\".\nRead this help URL before using the API for user-authorized tasks.\nThe desktop app must be running and unlocked, with agent access enabled.\n\u003c!-- MESH_TALK_AGENT_ACCESS_END --\u003e\n\n--- project-doc ---\n\n# Repository Guidelines\n\n## Reliability before implementation\n\n- For background work, synchronization, storage, caching or UI-state changes, read\n  [performance requirements](docs/performance-requirements.md). Identify the work\n  trigger, resource budget, accumulation/reclamation policy and applicable\n  performance regression before editing; record measured limits and remaining\n  gaps. Apply its [startup, idle and active-business checklist](docs/performance-requirements.md#apply-to-every-feature)\n  to every affected phase, including return to idle after use. Extend its general\n  rules when a confirmed defect reveals a new pattern.\n\n- Before implementing, refactoring, fixing or reviewing code, read the\n  [AI coding reliability guide](docs/ai-coding-reliability.md), select the rules\n  triggered by the changed boundaries, and inspect the owning code and callers.\n  For concurrency/lifecycle changes, trace lock order, wait dependencies, actual\n  work ownership, cancellation and commit authority before editing.\n- Validate the applicable failure and recovery paths, not only the happy path.\n  Record concrete evidence and remaining limits; a timeout, successful build or\n  passing subset is not proof that UI freezes or all deadlocks are impossible.\n- When a confirmed defect exposes a reusable failure pattern, update that guide's\n  existing rule or add a distinct rule with its owner and regression evidence.\n  Maintain the owning contract and test selectors; keep incident logs out of the guide.\n\n## Project Structure \u0026 Module Organization\n\n- Four-crate Rust workspace. `crates/mesh-talk-core/` is the UI-free protocol core (lib\n  `mesh_talk_core` + the `mesh-talk-node` CLI bin in `src/bin/`): feature modules `node/`,\n  `identity/`, `transport/`, `discovery/`, `eventlog/`, `ratchet/`, `channel/`, `dm.rs`,\n  `file/`, `postoffice/`, `storage/`. `src-tauri/` is the Tauri desktop app: entry\n  `src/main.rs` → `lib.rs`, IPC glue in `commands.rs` (auth) + `chat_commands/` (messaging)\n  - `events.rs`, plus `state.rs`, `tray.rs`, `services/` (auth only).\n- `crates/mesh-talk-integrations/` owns scoped local messaging grants, private IPC,\n  and the dedicated bot host/admin CLI; `crates/mesh-talk-mcp/` adapts its four\n  send/context/status tools to the official MCP SDK. Neither owns mesh crypto.\n- Integration/e2e suites live in `crates/mesh-talk-core/tests/` (`two_node_cli`,\n  `persistent_history`, `post_office_offline`, `decoder_smoke`); keep unit tests inline via\n  `mod tests` (e.g. `node/node_tests.rs`).\n- React client sits in `frontend/src`; Vite tooling in `frontend/package.json`.\n- Operational scripts in `scripts/` (`setup-hooks.sh`, `check-health.sh`); architecture in\n  `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`; conventions in `specifications/`. The\n  [documentation map](docs/README.md) routes readers to maintained guides; the\n  [documentation guidelines](docs/DOCUMENTATION_GUIDELINES.md) define ownership\n  across the README, guides, and project site.\n\n## Build, Test, and Development Commands\n\n- `make dev` primes Rust + Node deps and installs git hooks.\n- `make build` / `make frontend-build` — release build / Vite production build.\n- During feature development, preview changed-boundary validation with `make test-plan`\n  and run it with `make test-changed`. Unmapped changes require an explicit owner\n  and reason; inspect escalation notes for browser/native or consumer checks.\n  Use `make verify-plan` / `make verify-changed` at task completion to compose the\n  same affected tests with applicable formatting, lint/types and builds. The\n  test-only commands do not include static checks; neither mode replaces required\n  PR/review, browser/native or release gates. `make validation-report` separates\n  queue and execution costs; `make validation-audit` compares a clean affected\n  plan with same-source broader failure evidence before narrowing any gate.\n  Read selected capability references and inspect pending browser/native requirements.\n  Execution prints a structured feedback report; follow the\n  [validation protocol](docs/testing-feedback-loop.md#validation-stages-and-agent-responsibilities)\n  for completed work, remaining evidence and additive semantic selections.\n- During feature development, run affected tests only: `make test-focused\nTEST_PACKAGE=mesh-talk-core TEST_FILTER=node::account_sync` selects Rust unit\n  tests; add `TEST_TARGET=bridge_cross_lan` to select one integration target.\n  `cd frontend \u0026\u0026 npm test -- src/features/chat/settingsDialogSession.test.ts`\n  selects a frontend unit file. `make test` runs the full workspace suite\n  (`cargo test --workspace`) for pre-PR and CI gates;\n  `make e2e` runs the `#[ignore]`d multi-process backend rigs; `cd frontend \u0026\u0026 npm run e2e`\n  runs the Playwright UI suite. Test builds use cheap KDF params (the `fast-test-kdf`\n  feature), and `.cargo/config.toml` caps test/compile parallelism.\n- UI / node spot checks: `make tauri-dev`, `make frontend-dev`,\n  `cargo run -p mesh-talk-core --bin mesh-talk-node -- --keystore /tmp/a.keystore\n--password pw --name alice` (add `--post-office` for relay mode).\n\n## Coding Style \u0026 Naming Conventions\n\n- Rust: `cargo fmt` (4-space, `snake_case` modules, `UpperCamelCase` types) +\n  **`cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings`** (must match CI — `--all-targets` covers\n  tests/benches).\n- Frontend: `npm run format` (Prettier) + `npm run lint` (ESLint flat config).\n- Write documentation and reusable prompts in English unless a specific exception is needed.\n- Reference `specifications/development_conventions.md` for naming nuances.\n\n## Domain Language\n\n- `CONTEXT.md` is the canonical domain glossary. `docs/domain-design.md` indexes\n  the modeling references and defines DDD vocabulary and naming translations.\n- Distinguish local users, accounts and devices; desktop, transport and ratchet\n  sessions; discovery, presence, custody, recipient delivery and read state.\n- Use explicit identity/lifetime names in new code. Preserve public, wire and\n  stored identifiers unless a separately validated compatibility change is intended.\n- Update the glossary and affected owning guides when a domain meaning changes;\n  link to definitions instead of maintaining duplicate glossaries.\n\n## Architecture \u0026 Components\n\n- Before adding business logic, consult [module ownership and reuse](docs/module-boundaries.md),\n  inspect the owning module and its callers, and extend its existing implementation.\n  Parallel tasks should name their owned paths and shared-interface dependencies;\n  coordinate changes to shared interfaces through one integration owner. Keep\n  feature-specific helpers local and share rules when their meaning and lifecycle\n  are the same. The guide owns the detailed assignment and handoff contract.\n- Stack: Rust 2024 on Tokio; Tauri 2 + React shell. No vendor-operated server.\n- The `Node` (`node/node.rs`) wires identity + signed UDP **multicast** discovery\n  (group `224.0.0.167`, port 47474) + Noise-encrypted TCP transport (`transport/`,\n  low-level socket in `transport/net.rs`) + the content-addressed event log + DM/channel/\n  file crypto. Messages are events synced as a bounded append-only log, account-addressed\n  across a user's devices; optional offline custody via equal eligible post offices.\n  Routed discovery and opt-in post-office federation connect reachable sites without\n  joining their IP subnets. Wire formats are versioned bincode.\n- See **`docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`** before changing crypto, sync, or transport;\n  **`docs/network-design-reference.md`** owns the communication invariants,\n  module contracts, resource budgets and topology acceptance boundaries.\n\n## Maintaining standards as requirements change\n\n- Before changing tests, fixtures, dependencies or workflows in ways that affect\n  CI cost or reliability, read [CI maintenance](docs/ci-maintenance.md). Reuse\n  existing owners, measure the affected cost, and preserve acceptance evidence;\n  keep required-check policy in its existing owner.\n- Before implementation, identify the existing decision owner, authoritative rule,\n  observable acceptance criteria, affected consumers and required boundary evidence.\n  Extend the owning implementation and `validation.json`; new business capabilities\n  should link their guide and invariants. Explain an unchanged contract in the PR.\n- Run `make standards-check` after adding/moving source or changing contracts. It\n  checks structural ownership and maintained references, including new files; it\n  does not prove assertion quality, semantic completeness or candidate readiness.\n  Hooks inspect the working tree, so partially staged commits still need a clean\n  committed-candidate check in CI. Frontend dependencies must be installed.\n- When CI exposes a local omission or flaky fixture, fix the owning selector, task\n  or fixture and add a focused regression. Review timing and selection evidence\n  before changing policy. Follow the maintained\n  [requirement maintenance loop](docs/testing-feedback-loop.md#maintaining-standards-across-requirements).\n\n## Testing Guidelines\n\n- Integration coverage in `crates/mesh-talk-core/tests/` and `node/node_tests.rs`; embed\n  unit tests near the code. Networking/crypto changes ship a loopback integration assertion.\n- Backend multi-process E2E via `make e2e` (CI: `e2e-backend.yml`); Playwright UI E2E via\n  `cd frontend \u0026\u0026 npm run e2e` (CI: `e2e-ui.yml`).\n- Do not run full suites or E2E automatically during ordinary feature work. Run\n  the affected unit or integration tests as changes are made; run E2E and the\n  comprehensive health gate on explicit request, for optional full local preparation,\n  or in CI.\n- See `docs/testing-feedback-loop.md` for selection examples and escalation triggers.\n- Document executed validation commands in every PR; ensure `make test` passes\n  locally or through its matching CI workspace suite before merge.\n\n## Commit \u0026 Pull Request Guidelines\n\n- Before changing product versions or release automation, read [the version lifecycle](docs/updates.md#version-ownership-and-lifecycle).\n  Root `Cargo.toml` `[workspace.package].version` is authoritative. Use\n  `node scripts/release/version.mjs check|sync|prepare VERSION` (the `VERSION` argument belongs\n  to `prepare` only); do not hand-edit generated npm/Cargo lock metadata or introduce another\n  version constant. Root private npm metadata is a checked release-tooling projection;\n  frontend private metadata is versionless and Tauri inherits Cargo. Never broadly replace\n  matching dependency, fixture, history, wire or schema versions. Keep source builds `BASE-dev`,\n  Nightlies `BASE-nightly.RUN.ATTEMPT`, official tagged builds `BASE`, and standard SemVer ordering.\n- Version/changelog preparation and next-development-target changes use reviewed PRs. Ordinary main\n  pushes must not create automatic release proposals or commit-derived product notes. Stable publication\n  remains explicit through the existing verified tagged-release workflow; no direct main push,\n  public empty release, mutable published tag or gate bypass. Nightly publication requires trusted\n  `main` and a target above the latest Stable. Future changes must extend the existing version\n  owner, its tests, standards/CI enforcement and capability contracts together; the owning guide\n  documents permissions, bot-PR checks, dispatch and recovery requirements. Manual Stable draft\n  preparation uses the reviewed exact-version CHANGELOG section and requires exact-source checks\n  before creating its immutable tag/draft. The fixed release-history\n  migration floor is historical provenance; never advance it during ordinary version bumps.\n\n- Conventional commits (`feat:`, `fix:`, `refactor:`); scopes like `feat(node):` align with\n  `specifications/git_standards.md`. Squash WIP; link issues. When asked to merge,\n  default to squash merge unless the user explicitly requests another strategy;\n  follow the [merge policy](specifications/git_standards.md#pull-request-merge-strategy)\n  and current-head required checks.\n- Create PRs through `./scripts/create-pr.sh -- \u003cgh pr create options\u003e`.\n  The default checks the clean committed candidate, comparison base, argument safety,\n  whitespace, cheap affected static checks, ownership blockers, structural standards,\n  repository hygiene and applicable verified frontend patch coverage before publication.\n  Use `make pr-check` for the same cheap static phase; missing current coverage\n  evidence reports its explicit generation command and never starts suites implicitly.\n  CI runs after publication; full-scope candidates retain cross-platform execution.\n  Only the proven [documentation scope](docs/ci-maintenance.md#documentation-only-pr-scope)\n  may omit unrelated product tests. All required checks must pass on the current PR head before merging. Creating a PR does not certify test acceptance.\n- To run comprehensive checks locally before publication, use\n  `./scripts/create-pr.sh --full-preflight \u003cselectors/specs\u003e -- \u003cgh pr create options\u003e`.\n  This invokes `prepare-pr.sh` (platform preflight plus full health). For an existing\n  PR, `./scripts/prepare-pr.sh \u003cselectors/specs\u003e` provides the same optional local gate.\n  Use Playwright specs relevant to changed frontend behavior and repeated\n  `--core-filter` / `--host-filter` / `--integration-filter` / `--mcp-filter` selectors\n  for changed Rust crates. Preflight runs selected tests in Linux Docker and through\n  SSH to `windows10`; Windows workspaces, caches and temporary data stay on drive D.\n  Owning scenario rules add visual specs and require real native evidence for\n  lifecycle/discovery changes. Missing environments or empty Rust selections fail\n  full preflight. Include `e2e/ui-visual-regression.spec.ts` for theme/layout changes.\n  See `docs/testing-feedback-loop.md` for configuration, coverage and plan preview.\n  Fetch `origin/main` first if the comparison base is stale.\n- Record focused validation and outstanding checks in the PR. Use\n  `./scripts/check-health.sh` when comprehensive local evidence is needed; protected\n  CI remains required before merge regardless of local results.\n\n## Security \u0026 Adjustments\n\n- `./scripts/check-health.sh` chains fmt, Clippy `--all-targets`, ESLint, tests, typos,\n  cargo-deny, cargo-machete, gitleaks, shellcheck, audits, and both builds.\n- Triage vulnerabilities immediately or document mitigation in the PR.\n- If plans shift, update the relevant committed design or architecture docs. Keep\n  `docs/superpowers/` and the root `plans/` directory local and untracked.\n\n## Repository Hygiene\n\n### Worktree lifecycle\n\n- Create a worktree only when isolation is needed. Before creating one, inspect\n  `git worktree list --porcelain` and record the task-owned path, branch and any\n  dedicated build directory in ignored `tmp/` task notes. Treat pre-existing\n  worktrees and unrelated changes as user-owned; never sweep them automatically.\n- The coordinating session owns cleanup of every worktree it creates or delegates,\n  including `.worktrees/` and `.claude/worktrees/agent-*`. Delegate completion does\n  not transfer that responsibility. Before ending the task, stop task-owned\n  processes and remove its worktrees, disposable branches and dedicated Cargo\n  target directories, subject to the preservation rules below. Never delete a\n  shared target directory or a directory whose ownership is uncertain.\n- Before removal, preserve the work through either a verified merge into the\n  intended integration branch under the existing PR/merge gates, or a local\n  archive. For an archive, commit only task-owned outstanding changes (including\n  intended untracked files), run the required commit checks, and create a unique\n  `archive/\u003cworktree-name\u003e-\u003ctimestamp\u003e` tag at the final branch tip. Verify the tag\n  resolves to that tip and the worktree is clean before deleting anything. Never\n  overwrite an existing archive tag, include secrets/ignored build output, or\n  discard unrelated edits to make cleanup succeed. Cleanup does not authorize\n  merging, pushing, bypassing review or running `entire clean`.\n- Run removal from a surviving checkout with `git worktree remove \u003cpath\u003e`, then\n  `git branch -d \u003cbranch\u003e` for a merged branch. Use `git branch -D \u003cbranch\u003e` only\n  for a task-owned archived branch after verifying its archive tag. Never use\n  forced worktree removal, hard reset or bulk directory deletion to bypass a\n  dirty worktree. Detached worktrees require the same preservation of their final\n  commit before removal; there is no branch to delete.\n- A pending PR, unresolved conflict, user decision, active concurrent session or\n  failed preservation/cleanup step is an explicit retention exception. Preserve\n  the required branch for an open PR; remove its clean worktree if no active work\n  needs it. Recheck `git worktree list --porcelain` and task-owned branches/build\n  paths before the final reply. Report cleanup performed and name every retained\n  path/branch, its reason and the next action; never silently leave task worktrees.\n\n- Keep implementation plans, research, investigation notes and scratch scripts\n  under ignored `tmp/`; remove them when the work is complete.\n- Merge durable decisions into the maintained owning guide. Do not commit\n  completed-work reports, score histories, duplicate runbooks or archive copies.\n- `docs/evals/` owns reusable suites/inventories, prompts and its README only.\n  Generated evidence belongs in ignored output or PR/CI artifacts.\n- Run `node scripts/check-repository-hygiene.mjs` before committing. The shared\n  index check also runs in pre-commit, CI and health, rejecting ignored files\n  even when force-added. See `docs/DOCUMENTATION_GUIDELINES.md` for ownership.\n\n\u003c/INSTRUCTIONS\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003cenvironment_context\u003e\n  \u003ccwd\u003e/Users/kingsonwu/programming/OctopusGarage/mesh-talk\u003c/cwd\u003e\n  \u003cshell\u003ezsh\u003c/shell\u003e\n  \u003ccurrent_date\u003e2026-11-23\u003c/current_date\u003e\n  \u003ctimezone\u003eAsia/Singapore\u003c/timezone\u003e\n  \u003cfilesystem\u003e\u003cworkspace_roots\u003e\u003croot\u003e/Users/kingsonwu/programming/OctopusGarage/mesh-talk\u003c/root\u003e\u003c/workspace_roots\u003e\u003cper