... If you find an override environment variable, use the `browserExecutableFolder` and `userDataFolder` values as replacements for the corresponding values in `CreateCoreWebView2EnvironmentWithOptions` parameters. ... While not strictly overrides, additional environment variables may be set.\n\nThe `browserExecutableFolder`, `channelSearchKind`, `releaseChannels`, `userDataFolder` and `additionalBrowserArguments` of the `environmentOptions` may be overridden by values either specified in environment variables or in the registry.\n\nWhen creating a `WebView2Environment` the following environment variables are verified.\n\ntext\n\nCopy\n    \n    `WEBVIEW2_BROWSER_EXECUTABLE_FOLDER\n    WEBVIEW2_USER_DATA_FOLDER\n    WEBVIEW2_ADDITIONAL_BROWSER_ARGUMENTS\n    WEBVIEW2_CHANNEL_SEARCH_KIND\n    WEBVIEW2_RELEASE_CHANNELS\n    `\n\nIf you find an override environment variable, use the `browserExecutableFolder` and `userDataFolder` values as replacements for the corresponding values in `CreateCoreWebView2EnvironmentWithOptions` parameters. If `additionalBrowserArguments` is specified in environment variable or in the registry, it is appended to the corresponding values in `CreateCoreWebView2EnvironmentWithOptions` parameters. See `ReleaseChannels` on ICoreWebView2EnvironmentOptions for more details on how to construct the registry key and environment variable values.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGlobals | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-edge/webview2/reference/win32/webview2-idl?view=webview2-1.0.3967.48)\nciteturn26search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; You may specify the `userDataFolder` to change the default user data folder location for WebView2. ... If you find an override environment variable, use the `browserExecutableFolder` and `userDataFolder` values as replacements for the corresponding values in `CreateCoreWebView2EnvironmentWithOptions` parameters. ... While not strictly overrides, additio","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","guardian_metadata":{"client_authored":false,"history_sequence":15858,"model_search_state":"unused","sampled_through_sequence":15857},"id":"rs_0dca4931a04d8132016b03d8f09dd887d0a6f695323d4532a2","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a20706-0b66-75d1-a839-57280589689c"},"summary":[],"type":"reasoning"},{"call_id":"call_6d66a9cf12a0414fab0176ef39727e42","guardian_metadata":{"client_authored":false,"history_sequence":15859,"model_search_state":"unused","sampled_through_sequence":15857},"id":"ctc_0dca4931a04d8132016b03d8f2072087d099ca0a1204cd5263","input":"text(await tools.exec_command({cmd:`set -e\nd=$(cat tmp/req-011/windows-native-minimize-path); r=$(cat \"$d/remote-path\"); old=$(cat \"$d/reception-cursors-path\")\nh=$(mktemp -d \"$d/reception-isolated.XXXXXXXX\"); name=$(basename \"$h\")\nprintf '%s\\\\n' \"$h\" >\"$d/reception-isolated-path\"\ncp \"$old/\"*.ps1 \"$old/trace.mjs\" \"$h/\"\npython3 - \"$h\" \"$old\" <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport sys\np,old=map(Path,sys.argv[1:])\nf=p/'run-reception-window-state.ps1';s=f.read_text().replace(old.name+'-loaded',p.name+'-loaded').replace('43882','43884');s=s.replace('$loaded =', '$env:WEBVIEW2_USER_DATA_FOLDER = \"$PSScriptRoot/webview-fresh\"\\\\n$loaded =',1);f.write_text(s)\nPY\nssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=8 windows10 \"powershell.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -Command \\\\\"New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path '$r/$name' -Force | Out-Null\\\\\"\"\nscp -q -o BatchMode=yes \"$h/\"*.ps1 \"windows10:$r/$name/\"\nssh -N -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=8 -o ExitOnForwardFailure=yes -L 127.0.0.1:43885:127.0.0.1:43884 windows10 >\"$h/tunnel.log\" 2>&1 &\ntunnel=$!\ntrap 'kill -TERM \"$tunnel\" 2>/dev/null || true' EXIT\nssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=8 windows10 powershell.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -File \"$r/$name/launch-reception-window-state.ps1\" >\"$h