{"timestamp":"2026-11-23T06:29:32.260Z","ordinal":37981,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1feaf-24d9-7612-a284-664abd64a2c4","turn_id":"01a20717-dcf4-7e41-b6f1-ff0239d71e14","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-cba3c570-f53f-4151-8346-12b95f1da105","process_id":"57078","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '35,75p' docs/performance-requirements.md\nrg -n 'post.office|relay' scripts/evals/resource_benchmark.py docs/evals/README.md | head -24\nrg --files --no-ignore tmp/req-011 -g '*postoffice*' -g '!**/node_modules/**' -g '!**/target/**' | head -12"],"cwd":"file:///Users/kingsonwu/programming/OctopusGarage/mesh-talk","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '35,75p' docs/performance-requirements.md","name":"performance-requirements.md","path":"docs/performance-requirements.md"},{"type":"search","cmd":"rg -n 'post.office|relay' scripts/evals/resource_benchmark.py docs/evals/README.md","query":"post.office|relay","path":"resource_benchmark.py"},{"type":"list_files","cmd":"rg --files --no-ignore tmp/req-011 -g '*postoffice*' -g '!**/node_modules/**' -g '!**/target/**'","path":"req-011"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"resident memory, shared pages and live JS heap; on Linux use PSS for shared-page\nattribution. Logical I/O includes IPC/network activity and is not physical disk\nI/O; OS disk events also do not measure physical NAND write amplification. CPU alone does not establish battery/power efficiency: inspect wakeups, GPU,\nnetwork and actual energy where supported. Keep metrics collected in different\nwindows separate. Store measurements in PR/CI artifacts, not this guide.\n\nAn existing target miss requires recorded baseline and an owned follow-up; it is\nnot permission to worsen the path or silently raise its budget. A target change\nrequires comparable evidence and review. Migrations, catch-up and active media have\nseparate bounded work budgets and progress evidence, not an unlimited exception.\n\n## Headless post-office nodes\n\nApply the headless CPU and private-memory targets to the dedicated post-office\nprocess, including its attributable helpers. A relay stays available to receive\nconnections, serve mailbox requests and maintain required protocol leases while\nquiescent. Its steady idle work must remain bounded as retained ciphertext,\noffline participants and configured federation peers grow. An empty store alone\ncannot establish the retained-workload result.\n\n- At startup, restore only the indexes, authority and bounded cursors needed to\n  accept traffic. Keep migrations, backlog catch-up and recovery measurable\n  separately. Report time to accepting requests, peak memory and I/O.\n- In steady idle, wait for ingress, committed mailbox or topology changes and\n  the next real expiry, lease or retry deadline. Unchanged federation inventories,\n  empty drains and repeated unreachable peers must settle or back off. Sample\n  both an empty relay and a populated relay after warmup, including CPU, memory,\n  wakeups and disk writes. Observe the full hour to claim the write target.\n- Under traffic, measure delivery and custody latency, queue depth, memory,\n  CPU and I/O. Verify bounded batches, overload handling, restart recovery,\n  federation convergence and return to idle. Keep receipt meanings, retention,\n  authenticated scope and convergence guarantees intact.\n\nUse the existing [relay resource contracts](post-office-federation.md#indexed-relay-runtime-and-resource-bounds)\nand [event and scheduling owners](event-driven-design.md#schedule-work-by-purpose).\nRecord platform, binary, topology, load, sampling window and missing evidence\nalongside every result.\n\n## Apply to every feature\n\nBefore implementation, identify the owning producer, its meaningful trigger,\ndocs/evals/README.md:24:| MT-AI-006 | `docs/post-office-metadata-privacy.md` | Relay privacy claims match visible routing metadata, custody limits, key compromise, and V1 migration behavior.                                                                                                                     |\ndocs/evals/README.md:25:| MT-AI-007 | `docs/post-office-macos-service.md`    | Intel Mac service setup, credential handling, health checks, lifecycle limits, and separate cross-machine offer and file-byte relay verification.                                                                                   |\ndocs/evals/README.md:26:| MT-AI-008 | `docs/post-office-windows-service.md`  | Windows Scheduled Task setup, credential and lock handling, health checks, and separate cross-machine delivery layers.                                                                                                              |\ndocs/evals/README.md:28:| MT-AI-010 | `docs/routed-network-discovery.md`     | Equal enrolled post offices exchange eligible signed presence and recipient-encrypted mail with signed consent/completion; direct relay selection, onward routes, bounded reconciliation and custody/evidence limits stay explicit. |\ndocs/evals/README.md:31:It distinguishes equal-relay federation from the older pinned TCP gateway. It\ndocs/evals/README.md:88:| MT-SMOKE-002 | `make e2e`                                                     | Real `mesh-talk-node` two-node, persistent-history, post-office, channel, and file flows remain runnable. |\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":148237292}},"started_at_ms":1795415372111,"completed_at_ms":1795415372260}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-23T06:29:32.371Z","ordinal":37982,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a20718-7253-72c0-849d-b95afbd66fd1","call_id":"call_2361a77695d044d8b3ad2174d7da8ee3","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.5 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"74f8c1\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.14829,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":1034,\"output\":\"resident memory, shared pages and live JS heap; on Linux use PSS for shared-page\\nattribution. Logical I/O includes IPC/network activity and is not physical disk\\nI/O; OS disk events also do not measure physical NAND write amplification. CPU alone does not establish battery/power efficiency: inspect wakeups, GPU,\\nnetwork and actual energy where supported. Keep metrics collected in different\\nwindows separate. Store measurements in PR/CI artifacts, not this guide.\\n\\nAn existing target miss requires recorded baseline and an owned follow-up; it is\\nnot permission to worsen the path or silently raise its budget. A target change\\nrequires comparable evidence and review. Migrations, catch-up and active media have\\nseparate bounded work budgets and progress evidence, not an unlimited exception.\\n\\n## Headless post-office nodes\\n\\nApply the headless CPU and private-memory targets to the dedicated post-office\\nprocess, including its attributable helpers. A relay stays available to receive\\nconnections, serve mailbox requests and maintain required protocol leases while\\nquiescent. Its steady idle work must remain bounded as retained ciphertext,\\noffline participants and configured federation peers grow. An empty store alone\\ncannot establish the retained-workload result.\\n\\n- At startup, restore only the indexes, authority and bounded cursors needed to\\n  accept traffic. Keep migrations, backlog catch-up and recovery measurable\\n  separately. Report time to accepting requests, peak memory and I/O.\\n- In steady idle, wait for ingress, committed mailbox or topology changes and\\n  the next real expiry, lease or retry deadline. Unchanged federation inventories,\\n  empty drains and repeated unreachable peers must settle or back off. Sample\\n  both an empty relay and a populated relay after warmup, including CPU, memory,\\n  wakeups and disk writes. Observe the full hour to claim the write target.\\n- Under traffic, measure delivery and custody latency, queue depth, memory,\\n  CPU and I/O. Verify bounded batches, overload handling, restart recovery,\\n  federation convergence and return to idle. Keep receipt meanings, retention,\\n  authenticated scope and convergence guarantees intact.\\n\\nUse the existing [relay resource contracts](post-office-federation.md#indexed-relay-runtime-and-resource-bounds)\\nand [event and scheduling owners](event-driven-design.md#schedule-work-by-purpose).\\nRecord platform, binary, topology, load, sampling window and missing evidence\\nalongside every result.\\n\\n## Apply to every feature\\n\\nBefore implementation, identify the owning producer, its meaningful trigger,\\ndocs/evals/README.md:24:| MT-AI-006 | `docs/post-office-metadata-privacy.md` | Relay privacy claims match visible routing metadata, custody limits, key compromise, and V1 migration behavior.                                                                                                                     |\\ndocs/evals/README.md:25:| MT-AI-007 | `docs/post-office-macos-service.md`    | Intel Mac service setup, credential handling, health checks, lifecycle limits, and separate cross-machine offer and file-byte relay verification.                                                                                   |\\ndocs/evals/README.md:26:| MT-AI-008 | `docs/post-office-windows-service.md`  | Windows Scheduled Task setup, credential and lock handling, health checks, and separate cross-machine delivery layers.                                                                                                              |\\ndocs/evals/README.md:28:| MT-AI-010 | `docs/routed-network-discovery.md`     | Equal enrolled post offices exchange eligible signed presence and recipient-encrypted mail with signed consent/completion; direct relay selection, onward routes, bounded reconciliation and custody/evidence limits stay explicit. |\\ndocs/evals/README.md:31:It distinguishes equal-relay federation from the older pinned TCP gateway. It\\ndocs/evals/README.md:88:| MT-SMOKE-002 | `make e2e`                                                     | Real `mesh-talk-node` two-node, persistent-history, post-office, channel, and file flows remain runnable. |\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a20717-dcf4-7e41-b6f1-ff0239d71e14","create_time":1795415372.37189}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":16121,"sampled_through_sequence":16118,"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000,"model_search_state":"unused"}}
