         b11b319e [diagnostics/ci-file-source-20261103]\\n/Users/kingsonwu/programming/OctopusGarage/mesh-talk/.worktrees/req-029                                                                      0ae1a692 [req-029]\\n/Users/kingsonwu/programming/OctopusGarage/mesh-talk/.worktrees/system-evolution-20261019                                                    1050a83f [feat/system-evolution-20261019]\\n2. Follow evidence into REQ-017 (history and memory lifecycle), REQ-006\\n   (redundant processing and I/O), or REQ-005 (scheduling). Add the corresponding\\n   REQ-011 regression check. Build REQ-001 around verified metrics where useful.\\n3. REQ-010 is the first independent feature and the fallback if the initial\\n   profile yields no actionable hotspot. REQ-014 and REQ-003 are also independent.\\n4. Address REQ-004, REQ-009, REQ-022 and REQ-030 within the change that needs\\n   their terminology, documentation, rationale, or verification.\\n5. Use measurements to scope REQ-007 and REQ-012. Apply REQ-013 and REQ-018\\n   where a feature needs privacy or identity and permission guarantees.\\n6. Let actual consumers drive REQ-008 and REQ-015, then REQ-020 and REQ-021\\n   when an external consumer or second client has concrete requirements.\\n7. REQ-002 and REQ-023 can start independently after their own permission\\n   checks. Define REQ-019 propagation and retention before REQ-024 forwarding.\\n   REQ-026 has its own audience rules. Use REQ-025 before allowing stranger\\n   contact, validate REQ-027 pairing and browser feasibility before expansion,\\n   and defer REQ-028 until optional resources need allocation. Apply REQ-029\\n   to specific product decisions.\\n\\n\"}}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a20756-9c6d-7151-b9aa-d3f86228c963","create_time":1795419681.484151}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":17152,"sampled_through_sequence":17149,"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000,"model_search_state":"unused"}}
